"""Phase 5a copy, batch 3: capacity, group vs 1:1, no-shows, assistant coach. Original prose (ADR 0036)."""
from phase5_common import base, emit, s

D = {}

D["client-capacity-calculator"] = base(
    "client-capacity-calculator",
    title="Client capacity calculator for coaches",
    h1="Client capacity calculator",
    description="Work out how many coaching clients you can serve well from your weekly hours, time per client, admin overhead and buffer, with revenue at capacity.",
    intro="Enter your coaching hours, the minutes each client takes per week, your admin overhead and the buffer you keep free. See the most clients you can serve at that depth, your theoretical ceiling and, with a price, revenue at capacity.",
    q="How many coaching clients can I handle?",
    a="Take your weekly hours, remove admin time, divide by minutes per client, then leave headroom. With 30 hours, 20% admin and 30 minutes per client, you have 24 hours of coaching time: room for 48 clients. Keeping a 15% buffer, about 40 is sustainable. At €120 a month, that's €4,800 a month at capacity.",
    sections=[
        s("Why capacity matters",
          "Every coach has a limit. Past it, replies slow down, check-in feedback gets shorter, plans become generic and clients start to notice. Churn rises just as the business seems to be growing. Knowing your capacity in advance lets you plan for it: raise prices before you hit it, bring in help, add a group offer, or simply stop taking on clients until you have space.",
          "Capacity also connects your income goal to your calendar. If your goal needs 60 clients and your capacity is 40, no amount of marketing will close the gap without changing how you deliver."),
        s("How the calculation works",
          ("table", ["Step", "Formula", "Default"], [["Coaching time", "Weekly hours × (1 − admin %)", "30 × 0.8 = 24 hours"], ["Ceiling", "Coaching minutes ÷ minutes per client", "1,440 ÷ 30 = 48 clients"], ["Sustainable", "Ceiling × (1 − buffer %)", "48 × 0.85 ≈ 40 clients"], ["Revenue at capacity", "Sustainable × price", "40 × €120 = €4,800 a month"]]),
          "The ceiling is the most clients you could fit if every week went perfectly. The sustainable figure leaves headroom for onboarding new clients, busy weeks, sick days and clients who need extra time."),
        s("Typical ranges by coaching mode",
          ("table", ["Mode", "Time per client per week", "Typical sustainable clients"], [["1:1 in-person", "45 to 75 minutes", "about 15 to 30"], ["Hybrid", "30 to 60 minutes", "about 25 to 50"], ["Online async", "15 to 40 minutes", "about 40 to 80 or more"]]),
          "Treat these as rough guides. Your own number depends on the depth of your service, how much programming you reuse, how many clients need nutrition support and how much of the week goes on non-coaching work."),
        s("Worked examples",
          ("table", ["Coach", "Hours", "Minutes per client", "Admin", "Buffer", "Sustainable clients"], [["In-person trainer", "30", "60", "20%", "15%", "20"], ["Online coach, light touch", "30", "15", "20%", "15%", "81"], ["Hybrid coach", "25", "45", "25%", "10%", "22"], ["Online coach, standard", "30", "20", "20%", "15%", "61"]]),
          "The online coach with 15 minutes per client can technically hold 81 clients. Whether that is a good idea depends on whether 15 minutes is enough to coach them well. Many coaches who chase very high client counts find quality slips and churn rises."),
        s("Measuring minutes per client honestly",
          "Minutes per client is the input that matters most, and it's often underestimated. Include everything you do for a client each week: reviewing check-ins, writing feedback, adjusting programmes, building meal plans, answering messages, and any calls or sessions. Track it for a week or two for a few typical clients.",
          "Many coaches find that messaging alone takes 10 minutes or more per client per week, more than they expected. Some clients take far more time than others. Use an average across your typical client, and consider separate tiers for clients who want much more contact."),
        s("Admin overhead",
          "Admin is the time that keeps the business running but isn't spent coaching: sales calls, content, invoicing, email, bookkeeping and learning. For many coaches it's 20 to 30% of the working week, and more for those who create a lot of content or run their own marketing.",
          "Be realistic. If you spend eight hours a week on content and sales, that's over a quarter of a 30-hour week. Underestimating admin is a common reason coaches feel overworked even when their client count looks reasonable."),
        s("Why keep a buffer",
          "A buffer is space you deliberately keep free. It absorbs new client onboarding, which often takes two or three times a normal week's time; clients going through difficult periods who need more support; and your own sick days or holidays. Without a buffer, every unusual week pushes you over capacity.",
          "A buffer of 10 to 20% suits most coaches. Those with many new clients each month, or with clients who need a lot of support, may need more."),
        s("Raising capacity without losing quality",
          ("ul", [
              "**Templates and libraries:** reusable programmes, exercise libraries and meal plan templates you adapt rather than build from scratch.",
              "**Structured check-ins:** a consistent form makes reviewing faster and feedback clearer.",
              "**Batching:** reviewing check-ins at set times rather than as they arrive.",
              "**Clear messaging expectations:** agreed response times and channels reduce constant interruptions.",
              "**Group elements:** a group call or community for common questions.",
              "**Help:** an assistant coach for some clients or tasks.",
          ]),
          "Each of these lowers minutes per client or admin overhead, which raises capacity without cutting the attention clients feel."),
        s("Capacity and pricing",
          "If you are near capacity, raising prices is often better than adding clients. Higher prices increase revenue without increasing workload, and if a few clients leave, you free space for new clients at the new rate. The price increase calculator shows how much client loss a rise can absorb.",
          "A waiting list is a sign you are at capacity and that demand supports a higher price."),
        s("Capacity and your income goal",
          "Multiply sustainable clients by price to see revenue at capacity. If that falls short of your goal, you have three options: raise price, increase capacity through systems or help, or change your model, for example by adding a group programme. Knowing which gap you face makes the decision clearer.",
          "At the default 40 clients and €120 a month, capacity revenue is €4,800 a month, or €57,600 a year. If your goal is €80,000, you need either more capacity or a higher price; working harder within the same structure won't get you there."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How many online coaching clients can one coach handle?", "It depends on time per client. Many online coaches spending 15 to 40 minutes per client per week run about 40 to 80, but quality matters more than the number."),
        ("What counts as time per client?", "Everything you do for a client each week: check-in review, feedback, programme changes, meal plans, messages and calls."),
        ("Why keep a buffer?", "To absorb onboarding, difficult weeks, sick days and holidays without going over capacity."),
        ("How can I take more clients without working more?", "Use templates, structured check-ins, batching and clear messaging expectations, or bring in help."),
        ("What should I do when I reach capacity?", "Raise prices, start a waiting list, add a group offer or bring in an assistant coach."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Assistant coach ROI calculator", "/tools/assistant-coach-roi-calculator"), ("Group vs 1:1 coaching calculator", "/tools/group-vs-1on1-coaching-calculator"), ("Price increase calculator", "/tools/price-increase-calculator")],
)

D["group-vs-1on1-coaching-calculator"] = base(
    "group-vs-1on1-coaching-calculator",
    title="Group vs 1:1 coaching calculator",
    h1="Group vs 1:1 coaching calculator",
    description="Compare the effective hourly rate, monthly revenue and weekly hours of one-to-one coaching and group coaching side by side.",
    intro="Enter the price, clients and hours for your one-to-one coaching and a group offer. See the effective hourly rate, monthly revenue and weekly hours for each, and which model earns more for your time.",
    q="Does group coaching pay more than one-to-one?",
    a="Often, per hour. Fifteen 1:1 clients at €400 a month taking an hour a week each bring €6,000 for 15 hours: about €92 an hour. A group of 30 at €150 taking 4 hours a week brings €4,500 for 4 hours: about €260 an hour. Effective hourly rate is monthly revenue ÷ (weekly hours × 4.33).",
    sections=[
        s("Two ways to sell your time",
          "One-to-one coaching sells your attention to one person at a time. It commands a higher price per client, because the plan, feedback and contact are all personal. Group coaching sells your attention to many people at once. Each pays less, but the same hour of your time serves more clients. The question isn't which is better; it's which suits your goals, your clients and your week.",
          "Comparing effective hourly rates puts the two on the same scale. It shows what each hour of coaching actually earns, which is often very different from what the price per client suggests."),
        s("The calculation",
          ("table", ["Measure", "1:1", "Group"], [["Price per client", "€400", "€150"], ["Clients", "15", "30"], ["Monthly revenue", "€6,000", "€4,500"], ["Weekly hours", "15 (1 h × 15)", "4"], ["Effective hourly rate", "€6,000 ÷ (15 × 4.33) ≈ €92", "€4,500 ÷ (4 × 4.33) ≈ €260"]]),
          "Effective hourly rate divides monthly revenue by monthly hours, using 4.33 weeks per month. It measures delivery hours only; marketing and admin are the same for both models and are left out."),
        s("More examples",
          ("table", ["Scenario", "1:1 hourly", "Group hourly", "Group revenue"], [["Group of 15 at €150, 3 hours a week", "€92", "€173", "€2,250"], ["Group of 30 at €150, 4 hours a week", "€92", "€260", "€4,500"], ["Group of 50 at €99, 5 hours a week", "€92 (20 clients at €300, 45 min)", "€228", "€4,950"]]),
          "Group coaching usually wins on hourly rate once the group is reasonably full. A small group, or one that takes many hours, may earn less per hour than one-to-one. The calculator makes the break-even point obvious."),
        s("What group coaching gives clients",
          "Group clients get structure, accountability and a community of people working towards similar goals. Many value hearing others' questions and wins, and feel less alone. They get less personal attention than one-to-one clients, which suits some people and not others.",
          "Groups work best when members share a goal and a starting point: beginners learning to lift, women returning to exercise after pregnancy, runners training for the same race. Mixed groups with very different needs are harder to coach well."),
        s("What one-to-one gives clients",
          "One-to-one clients get a fully personal plan, detailed feedback and direct access to you. That suits people with specific goals, injuries, complex lives or high expectations, and those willing to pay for individual attention. It also tends to produce stronger relationships and, often, longer retention.",
          "Many clients who start in a group later move to one-to-one coaching, and some one-to-one clients move into a group when they want ongoing support at a lower price. Offering both creates a path for clients to stay with you as their needs change."),
        s("Hidden time in group coaching",
          "Group hours aren't only the live calls. There's preparation, community moderation, answering questions in a group chat, and individual check-ins if you include them. A group that looks like 4 hours a week may really take 7 or 8. Count all of it in the calculator, or the hourly rate will look better than it is.",
          "Equally, count all the time in one-to-one coaching: messaging, programming and check-ins as well as sessions."),
        s("Group size and quality",
          "As a group grows, hourly rate rises, but each member gets less attention. Beyond a certain size, members may feel anonymous and engagement drops, which can increase churn. Many coaches find a sweet spot between 10 and 30 members for groups with regular live contact, and larger numbers for programmes built mostly around recorded content and community.",
          "Watch retention as the group grows. A group with high churn has to keep recruiting just to stay the same size."),
        s("Running both models",
          "Many coaches run a mix: a small number of premium one-to-one clients and one or two groups. The one-to-one clients bring high revenue per client and deep relationships; the groups bring high hourly rates and reach people who can't afford one-to-one. Enter each part into the calculator to see how much each contributes and how many hours each takes.",
          "A mix also spreads risk. If one-to-one demand dips, the group keeps income steady, and the other way round."),
        s("Pricing a group",
          "Group prices are usually a fraction of one-to-one prices, often a quarter to a half. Price from the value members get and the number needed to fill the group, not just from your time. A group that needs 30 members to earn your target rate is harder to fill than one that needs 12 at a higher price.",
          "Test with a small founding group at an introductory price, then raise the price once you've proven results and have testimonials."),
        s("Choosing your next step",
          "If your one-to-one calendar is full and your hourly rate is below your target, a group can raise income without more hours. If your group struggles to fill, or members want more personal help, one-to-one may be the better focus. Use the calculator with real or realistic numbers for both, and compare not only hourly rate but total revenue and the kind of coaching you want to do."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("Does group coaching earn more than 1:1?", "Often per hour, once the group is reasonably full. A small group or one that takes many hours may earn less per hour."),
        ("How do I calculate effective hourly rate?", "Monthly revenue ÷ (weekly hours × 4.33)."),
        ("How many people should be in a coaching group?", "Many coaches find 10 to 30 works for groups with regular live contact. Larger groups suit content-led programmes."),
        ("How should I price group coaching?", "Often a quarter to a half of your one-to-one price, depending on the support included and the group size you can fill."),
        ("Can I offer both group and 1:1?", "Yes. Many coaches run both, which creates a path for clients and spreads risk."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Client capacity calculator", "/tools/client-capacity-calculator"), ("Coaching package pricing calculator", "/tools/coaching-package-pricing-calculator"), ("Coaching revenue projector", "/tools/coaching-revenue-projector")],
)

D["no-show-cost-calculator"] = base(
    "no-show-cost-calculator",
    title="No-show cost calculator for coaches and trainers",
    h1="No-show cost calculator",
    description="Calculate the revenue you lose each week, month and year to no-shows and late cancellations, and the share of your calendar they take.",
    intro="Enter weekly no-shows, late cancellations you can't refill, your average session value and booked sessions per week. See revenue lost per week, month and year, the share of capacity lost and what halving the problem would win back.",
    q="How much do no-shows cost a personal trainer?",
    a="Multiply lost sessions by session value. Two no-shows and one late cancellation a week at €40 is €120 a week, about €520 a month and €6,240 a year, 10% of a 30-session week. Halving them wins back about €260 a month.",
    sections=[
        s("Why no-shows matter",
          "A missed session isn't only one lost payment. It's a slot you held, prepared for and can't usually fill at short notice. For trainers who are paid per session, it's direct lost income. For those on retainers, it's lost value for the client, which often leads to lower results, lower satisfaction and eventually cancellation.",
          "Because no-shows are spread across the week, they're easy to overlook. Adding them up for a year usually surprises coaches. A few missed sessions a week can add up to thousands."),
        s("The calculation",
          ("table", ["Measure", "Formula", "Default"], [["Lost sessions per week", "No-shows + late cancellations", "2 + 1 = 3"], ["Lost per week", "Lost sessions × session value", "3 × €40 = €120"], ["Lost per month", "Weekly × 4.33", "≈ €520"], ["Lost per year", "Weekly × 52", "€6,240"], ["Capacity lost", "Lost sessions ÷ booked sessions", "3 ÷ 30 = 10%"]]),
          "Only count late cancellations you can't refill. If a client cancels the day before and you book someone else into the slot, it isn't lost revenue."),
        s("More examples",
          ("table", ["Scenario", "Lost per week", "Lost per year", "Capacity lost"], [["1 no-show, 1 late cancel, €60 sessions, 25 booked", "€120", "€6,240", "8%"], ["2 no-shows, 1 late cancel, €40 sessions, 30 booked", "€120", "€6,240", "10%"], ["4 no-shows, 2 late cancels, €50 sessions, 40 booked", "€300", "€15,600", "15%"]]),
          "Even a low no-show rate adds up over a year. At 15% capacity lost, the third trainer is losing the equivalent of nearly two months of work."),
        s("Session value on a retainer",
          "If clients pay a monthly fee rather than per session, divide the fee by sessions per month to get session value. A client paying €320 for eight sessions has a session value of €40. Their no-show doesn't change your income that month, but it wastes the slot and lowers the value they get, which affects retention.",
          "For retainer clients, the true cost of no-shows is often churn: clients who miss sessions see fewer results and are more likely to cancel."),
        s("Why clients miss sessions",
          ("ul", [
              "**Forgetting:** no reminder, or a reminder too early to be useful.",
              "**Low commitment:** the session feels optional, especially if there's no cost to missing it.",
              "**Schedule clashes:** work, family or travel the client didn't plan around.",
              "**Low motivation:** a plateau, a bad week, or uncertainty about the plan.",
              "**Illness or emergencies:** unavoidable, and best handled kindly.",
          ]),
          "Most of these can be reduced with clear policies, good reminders and a coaching relationship that makes sessions feel worth showing up for."),
        s("Reducing no-shows",
          ("ol", [
              "Send a reminder the day before and a short message an hour before.",
              "Set and share a clear cancellation policy, such as 24 hours' notice.",
              "Use packages paid in advance, so sessions feel committed.",
              "Book recurring slots, so sessions become a habit.",
              "Follow up after a missed session with a friendly message, not a telling-off.",
              "Notice patterns: a client who misses two in a row may be losing motivation.",
          ]),
          "Halving no-shows and late cancellations, which is realistic for many trainers, wins back half the annual loss."),
        s("Cancellation policies",
          "A cancellation policy sets expectations: how much notice clients need to give, and what happens if they don't. Common policies include counting late cancellations as used sessions, charging a fee, or allowing a set number of late changes per month. Whatever you choose, explain it at the start, put it in writing and apply it consistently.",
          "Be fair and flexible for genuine emergencies. A rigid policy applied harshly can cost you a client worth far more than one session."),
        s("No-shows in online coaching",
          "Online coaches usually have fewer live sessions, but the same idea applies to scheduled calls and to missed check-ins. A client who misses a weekly check-in isn't costing you a slot, but they're losing momentum. Track missed check-ins the same way: count them, notice patterns and reach out quickly. They are among the clearest early signs a client may leave."),
        s("Turning no-show time into value",
          "When a session is missed, the slot doesn't have to be wasted. Some trainers keep a short list of clients who want extra sessions or would take an earlier slot, and message them when a gap appears. Others use the time for programming, content or admin. Either way, the slot does something useful even if the revenue is lost."),
        s("Tracking no-shows",
          "Keep a simple record: date, client, no-show or late cancellation, and reason if known. After a month, enter the weekly averages into the calculator. After a quarter, look for patterns by client, day and time. Fixing a handful of repeat offenders, or moving a troublesome time slot, often reduces no-shows more than general policies."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How do I calculate the cost of no-shows?", "Multiply lost sessions per week by session value, then by 4.33 for a month or 52 for a year."),
        ("Should late cancellations count?", "Only those you can't refill. If you rebook the slot, it isn't lost revenue."),
        ("What is a good cancellation policy for personal trainers?", "Many use 24 hours' notice, with late cancellations counted as used sessions. Apply it consistently and kindly."),
        ("How can I reduce no-shows?", "Reminders the day before and an hour before, a clear policy, prepaid packages and recurring slots."),
        ("What about clients on monthly retainers?", "Divide the monthly fee by sessions to get session value. Missed sessions lower results and often lead to churn."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Client churn rate calculator", "/tools/client-churn-rate-calculator"), ("Client capacity calculator", "/tools/client-capacity-calculator"), ("Coaching package pricing calculator", "/tools/coaching-package-pricing-calculator")],
)

D["assistant-coach-roi-calculator"] = base(
    "assistant-coach-roi-calculator",
    title="Assistant coach ROI calculator",
    h1="Assistant coach ROI calculator",
    description="See the margin you keep and the hours you free by handing clients to an assistant coach, paid per client, by share or by salary.",
    intro="Enter what clients pay, how many you'd hand over, how much time each takes and how you'd pay an assistant: a flat fee per client, a share of the fee or a monthly salary. See the margin you keep, hours freed, the salary break-even and the price rise that would earn the same without hiring.",
    q="Is hiring an assistant coach worth it?",
    a=["Compare the margin you keep with the hours you free. Handing 15 clients at €200 to an assistant paid €60 per client keeps €2,100 a month, €140 per client, and frees 7.5 hours a week at half an hour per client. If a platform takes 5% of payments, the margin falls to €1,950.",
       "To earn the same €2,100 without hiring, you'd need to raise each of your 40 clients by about €52.50 a month."],
    sections=[
        s("Why coaches hire assistants",
          "At some point, many coaches hit a ceiling: they have more demand than time. An assistant coach lets them take on more clients, or free their own time for higher-value work such as sales, content, premium clients or simply rest. The assistant handles some clients day to day, with the head coach overseeing programmes and quality.",
          "The trade-off is margin. Each handed-off client brings in the same fee but costs you the assistant's pay. The question is whether the margin you keep, plus the time you free, is worth more than coaching those clients yourself."),
        s("How the calculation works",
          ("table", ["Measure", "Formula", "Default"], [["Revenue from handed-off clients", "Price × clients handed off", "€200 × 15 = €3,000"], ["Assistant cost (flat)", "Fee per client × clients", "€60 × 15 = €900"], ["Platform fee", "Revenue × fee %", "€0 with no revenue share"], ["Margin you keep", "Revenue − assistant − platform", "€2,100"], ["Hours freed", "Hours per client × clients", "0.5 × 15 = 7.5 a week"]]),
          "The price-rise comparison spreads the same margin across all your current clients: €2,100 ÷ 40 = €52.50 per client per month, about 26% on a €200 price."),
        s("Three ways to pay an assistant",
          ("table", ["Pay model", "Default", "Margin on 15 clients at €200"], [["Flat fee per client", "€60 per client per month", "€2,100"], ["Share of the fee", "40% of each client's fee", "€1,800"], ["Monthly salary", "€3,000 a month", "−€150 with a 5% platform fee"]]),
          "Flat fees and shares scale with the number of clients handed off, so the risk is low. A salary is fixed, so it only pays off once enough clients are handed over. With a 5% platform fee, each client nets €190, and you'd need 16 clients to cover a €3,000 salary."),
        s("The salary break-even",
          "For a salaried assistant, the salary break-even is the number of handed-off clients needed to cover the salary: salary ÷ net revenue per client. At €2,500 a month with no platform fee and €200 per client, that's 13 clients. Handing over 20 clients would leave €1,500 a month of margin and free 10 hours a week.",
          "Salaried assistants make most sense once you have a steady flow of clients to hand over, and when you want an assistant who is committed to your business full time."),
        s("Counting platform and payment fees",
          "If your coaching platform or payment processor takes a share of client payments, it reduces margin on every handed-off client. A 5% fee on €3,000 is €150 a month. Choose 'Platform takes a share' and enter the percentage to include it. Card processing fees typically apply on top of any platform share; include them in the same percentage if you want a fuller picture."),
        s("What the freed hours are worth",
          "The calculator shows hours freed per week, but their value depends on what you do with them. If you use 7.5 hours to take on more premium clients, run sales calls or build a group programme, the return may be far higher than the margin you gave up. If the hours disappear into email and admin, the handoff mainly costs you money.",
          "Before hiring, decide what the freed time is for, and set a target, such as five new premium clients within three months."),
        s("Raising prices instead",
          "The 'raise prices instead' figure shows the alternative: earning the same margin by charging all your current clients more, without hiring. In the default example, that's about €52.50 more per client per month, a 26% increase. For some coaches, a smaller price rise plus fewer clients is a better route to the same income with less management.",
          "The two aren't mutually exclusive. Many coaches raise prices and hire, so that the assistant's clients are more profitable from the start."),
        s("Keeping quality high",
          ("ul", [
              "**Write down your method:** how you programme, how you review check-ins, how you respond to common questions.",
              "**Start small:** hand over a few clients first, review the assistant's work closely, then expand.",
              "**Review regularly:** read a sample of check-in replies each week.",
              "**Stay visible:** a monthly call or message from you keeps handed-off clients connected to your brand.",
              "**Track churn:** compare retention for handed-off clients with your own.",
          ]),
          "If handed-off clients leave faster, the margin you keep shrinks quickly. Quality is the foundation of the whole calculation."),
        s("Which clients to hand over",
          "Hand over clients who are well established, need standard support and fit the assistant's strengths. Keep clients with complex needs, high-value packages or close relationships with you. Tell clients about the change honestly and explain the benefits: more frequent contact, faster replies, the same programme philosophy."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What's a fair way to pay an assistant coach?", "It varies. Common models are a flat fee per client, a share of each client's fee, or a salary. Use the calculator to see your margin for each."),
        ("How many clients should I hand over?", "Start with a few, review quality, then expand. For a salaried assistant, at least enough to cover the salary break-even."),
        ("What is the salary break-even?", "Salary ÷ net revenue per handed-off client: the number of clients needed to cover the salary."),
        ("Should I raise prices instead of hiring?", "Sometimes. The calculator shows the price rise that would earn the same margin without hiring; many coaches do both."),
    ],
    nxt=[("Client capacity calculator", "/tools/client-capacity-calculator"), ("Coaching profit calculator", "/tools/coaching-profit-calculator"), ("Price increase calculator", "/tools/price-increase-calculator")],
)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    from phase5_a3_extra import EXTRA

    emit(D, EXTRA)
